{"date": "2026-10-30", "post_id": 1506568, "title": "Haunting the Cepheus Flare", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://exoplanets.nasa.gov/alien-worlds/galaxy-of-horrors/\">Spooky</a> shapes seem to haunt this dusty expanse, drifting through the night in the royal constellation <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cepheus_(constellation)\">Cepheus</a>. Of course, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-october-30-a-rorschach-aurora/\">the shapes</a> are cosmic dust clouds visible in dimly reflected starlight. Far from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/stories/\">your own neighborhood</a>, they lurk above the plane of the Milky Way at the edge of the <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4761\">Cepheus Flare</a> molecular cloud complex some 1,200 light-years away. Over 2 light-years across and brighter than most of the other ghostly apparitions, vdB 141 or Sh2-136 is also known as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=111031\">Ghost Nebula</a>. It lies at lower right in the starry field of view. Inside the Ghost Nebula are reddish telltale signs of dense cores collapsing in the early stages <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/exploring-the-birth-of-stars/\">of star formation</a>. With the eerie hue of interstellar dust reflecting bluish light from hot young stars of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=210903\">NGC 7023, the Iris Nebula</a> stands out against the dark just left of center. <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/mirko75?i=20obtn\">In the broad telescopic frame</a>, these fertile interstellar dust fields stretch almost six full moons across the sky.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>night on a spooky planet", "credit": "<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/mirko75\">Mirko Tondinelli</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/mirko75\">Mirko Tondinelli</a>", "alt": "Interstellar dust clouds and dark nebulae in the Cepheus molecular cloud.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg?w=6000&h=4076&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 30 - Haunting the Cepheus Flare</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Spooky shapes seem to haunt this dusty expanse, drifting through the night in the royal constellation Cepheus. Of course, the shapes are cosmic dust clouds visible in dimly reflected starlight. Far from your own neighborhood, they lurk above the plane of the Milky Way at the edge of the Cepheus Flare molecular cloud complex some 1,200 light-years away. Over 2 light-years across and brighter than most of the other ghostly apparitions, vdB 141 or Sh2-136 is also known as the Ghost Nebula. It lies at lower right in the starry field of view. Inside the Ghost Nebula are reddish telltale signs of dense cores collapsing in the early stages of star formation. With the eerie hue of interstellar dust reflecting bluish light from hot young stars of NGC 7023, the Iris Nebula stands out against the dark just left of center. In the broad telescopic frame, these fertile interstellar dust fields stretch almost six full moons across the sky.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: night on a spooky planet\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"haunting the cepheus flare\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Haunting the Cepheus Flare\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4076&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4076&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Interstellar dust clouds and dark nebulae in the Cepheus molecular cloud.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-30T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-29T16:58:22-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Haunting the Cepheus Flare\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4076&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 30<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/CepheusFlare_Tondinelli.jpg\" alt=\"Interstellar dust clouds and dark nebulae in the Cepheus molecular cloud.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Haunting the Cepheus Flare </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/mirko75\">Mirko Tondinelli</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <a href=\"https://exoplanets.nasa.gov/alien-worlds/galaxy-of-horrors/\">Spooky</a> shapes seem to haunt this dusty expanse, drifting through the night in the royal constellation <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cepheus_(constellation)\">Cepheus</a>. Of course, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-october-30-a-rorschach-aurora/\">the shapes</a> are cosmic dust clouds visible in dimly reflected starlight. Far from <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/stories/\">your own neighborhood</a>, they lurk above the plane of the Milky Way at the edge of the <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4761\">Cepheus Flare</a> molecular cloud complex some 1,200 light-years away. Over 2 light-years across and brighter than most of the other ghostly apparitions, vdB 141 or Sh2-136 is also known as the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=111031\">Ghost Nebula</a>. It lies at lower right in the starry field of view. Inside the Ghost Nebula are reddish telltale signs of dense cores collapsing in the early stages <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/science-highlights/exploring-the-birth-of-stars/\">of star formation</a>. With the eerie hue of interstellar dust reflecting bluish light from hot young stars of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=210903\">NGC 7023, the Iris Nebula</a> stands out against the dark just left of center. <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/mirko75?i=20obtn\">In the broad telescopic frame</a>, these fertile interstellar dust fields stretch almost six full moons across the sky.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>night on a spooky planet\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261030/html"}